00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.550
of Google's comprehensive
approach to security

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.480
and help you better understand
how your own business on top

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.890
of GCP benefits and can
leverage all the security

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:13.520
work that we have done
over the last 10 years.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.820
Before I get started, let's talk
a little bit about the threats

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:19.880
that we are facing
in today's world.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.340
Unfortunately, we
continue to live in a time

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.730
where companies end up getting
compromised frequently.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:29.640
If I pick up a newspaper
and see another story

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.600
of a large compromise, I'm
no longer really surprised.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.030
The scope of these compromises
can be quite large.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:40.050
You may be remembering
Yahoo in 2013.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.020
They lost 1 billion accounts.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.650
The sophistication of the
adversaries is also very high.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.000
It includes
state-sponsored actors

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.890
with corresponding resources.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.760
Just think back to
the 2016 elections,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.880
where the Russians
compromised the DNC.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.780
Unfortunately, this
is not really a trend

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.762
that is unlikely to stop.

00:02:06.762 --> 00:02:08.470
I mean, this is not
a trend that's likely

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.160
going to stop anytime soon.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.850
So you know, the natural
question that I ask myself,

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.670
and that you might also
be asking yourself is,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.040
what should we do about that?

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.640
Many companies these days
have started to realize that

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.250
security cannot be ignored.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:28.390
And that building an effective
security engineering team

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:32.050
is something that they have to
do, but is quite challenging.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:35.460
And it's challenging because
not only is it very expensive.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.950
There's also a fierce
competition for the talent.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.760
So in my perhaps
somewhat biased opinion,

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.430
the cloud, where
you can build on top

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.880
of professionally managed
and secure infrastructure,

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.330
is really the only
viable solution.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:55.180
As you will see
for Google's cloud,

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.950
we are focused on designing
in defense in depth

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.209
throughout out the stack.

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:01.750
And that's what I
want to talk to you

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.460
about through the
remainder of this talk.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:12.020
We strongly believe
that trust needs

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.750
to be gained through
transparency.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.240
And for that reason,
we recently released

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.020
the Google infrastructure
security design overview.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.370
And this paper discussed how
we secure our infrastructure.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.310
And also, explains
our holistic approach

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.580
to the entire information
processing lifecycle.

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.070
And in this talk
here, I will closely

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.710
follow the material
in that paper.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:38.790
And if you have not
done so already,

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.120
I hope that you go look up
that paper after the session

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:41.990
and read it.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:43.730
Because there's a
lot more information

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.690
contained in there than I
can present here in an hour.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.120
The security of
our infrastructure

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.780
is designed into all
layers of the platform,

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.020
starting from the physical
security of our data centers,

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.860
the security of our
purpose-built hardware,

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.900
the security of
the software stack,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:06.320
ranging all the way to
the technical constraints

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.020
and processes that
form the basis

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.420
of our operational security.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:17.649
To make it easier for you to
read the paper later and relate

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:21.130
it to this talk, let
me explain our layers

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.140
in slightly more detail.

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:25.690
As many of you may be used
to the layers of a more

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:29.740
traditional stack, I will
now explain why and how

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:33.140
our layers map onto it.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.260
Google's security approach
stems from our focus

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.100
to deliver global
services at scale.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.890
We do, however, understand
that many of our customers

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.530
and businesses may be
at different points

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.100
in their journey to deploying
all of their services

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.390
as internet services.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.960
And as a result, some of you may
have a different stack mapping.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.830
For example, we do
not treat the network

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.260
as our primary perimeter.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.420
It's not our security barrier.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.780
And that may be true
for companies in a more

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.300
traditional perimeter model.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.650
At the first layer, the
hardware infrastructure for us

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.320
includes all the
underlying pieces

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.990
that go into securely
booting the hardware.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:26.140
The service deployment
layer results from the fact

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.180
that Google's whole
infrastructure

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:31.810
is based on the notion
of separate services

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:35.020
that need to interact
with each other.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:39.280
Our need to securely conduct
service deployment results

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.080
in strict requirements for
low-level machine end service

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.820
identities.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.960
This means that our services run
on top of a curated operating

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:53.680
system, and that all
inter-process communication

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.010
is mutually authenticated.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:00.490
At the data storage level,
we have multiple layers

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.310
of storage and file
system abstractions

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.100
above the physical
layer that are widely

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.940
distributed for availability
and reliability.

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:13.330
Security properties
include encryption at rest

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:17.660
as well as automated
deletion of data.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:21.350
As Google primarily
delivers internet services,

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:25.130
securing internet communication
results in requirements

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.650
that span across the
network application

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:30.620
and deployment layers of
the traditional stack.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:34.610
It also leads to an integrated
design across these layers,

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.820
such as deploying central
enforcement points that

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:41.450
provide services, such
as TLS termination

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:45.870
or protection against
denial of service attacks.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:49.440
Similarly, securing
user identities

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.280
also drives requirements
and integrated design

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.420
across the application,
deployment, and usage layers.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:04.110
Lastly, operational security
requires special capabilities

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:05.910
not only from the
application layer,

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.630
but also from the operations and
usage layers, such as providing

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.370
context-rich telemetry.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.150
However, we also understand
that we can never rest

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:18.580
and that we need to review
our approach to security

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.110
constantly.

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:21.670
And for that reason,
we believe it

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:26.200
is important to be able to
think and act like an adversary.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:30.880
For example, we conduct our own
internal [INAUDIBLE] exercises.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.310
And we also have a
strong track record

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:35.470
in finding critical
vulnerabilities

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.660
in widely-deployed software.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:42.010
And we use this understanding
to build principal defenses.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:45.850
So in summary, to gain the
full measure of defense

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:50.080
in depth within Google, we
have design capabilities that

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:54.580
by necessity span and integrate
across multiple layers

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.810
of a more traditional stack.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:02.400
The result for us is that we
protect both Google and Google

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:05.730
Cloud customers through this
defense in depth approach.

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.550
The homogeneity of
our infrastructure

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.400
and our integrated and
comprehensive stance

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.210
on security reduces
complexity and allows

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:20.160
us to deliver security
by default and at scale.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.340
Through the remainder
of this talk,

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:25.350
I will provide more details
on Google's unique approach

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:29.100
to security and cover
which security features are

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.740
available to US
customers by default.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:35.340
Those that are available to
you as distinct products.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.809
And also, what is available
via partner solutions.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:42.100
Don't be overwhelmed by this.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.630
Don't try to read it.

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:45.190
I'll go through some
of these details

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:46.840
in the remainder of the talk.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.180
So if we were to
map all the security

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.990
capabilities and the
features of Google's platform

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:57.180
against a traditional stack,
this is how it would look like.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.200
You can see the
security capabilities

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:05.140
at each layer that combine into
our overall security stance.

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:08.170
As I mentioned
before, GCP is built

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:11.470
on top of Google's secure
infrastructure foundation,

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.280
which underlies all Google
Cloud offerings as well

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.330
as our consumer products,
such as Gmail or Photos.

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:24.780
The security of the core
infrastructure foundation

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:29.300
is something that Google
secures and manages by default.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:33.230
Based on our own experience,
building our own internal cloud

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:37.100
platform, we continue to
externalize the existing

00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:40.310
security capabilities
of our internal platform

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:43.850
so that you can secure your
own workloads with proven

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:46.100
technologies.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:48.080
While I will not go
into any details,

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:49.880
when you look at the
stack later, please

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.030
pay attention to the key in
the upper right-hand corner.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:56.330
It highlights the security the
platform provides by default,

00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:58.640
the security that you
get via Google products,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.310
as well as the security
solutions offered by partners.

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:06.800
And also note as
we go forward, we

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:10.580
are highlighting whether the
capabilities in each slide

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:14.030
is available to you by default
on our platform or as a product

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:16.520
that you can
configure and leverage

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.820
by the color box in the upper
right-hand corner of the slide.

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:21.970
So let's talk about hardware.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:25.130
Due to Google scale,
we have the luxury

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:29.120
of designing and building
our own data centers.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.030
These data centers
incorporate multiple layers

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:34.580
of physical security
and access to them

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:38.250
is limited to a very small
fraction of Google employees.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.330
Again, we practice
defense in depth

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:45.500
by employing multiple physical
security layers to protect

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.180
our data center floors.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:49.190
These technologies
include things

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.620
such as biometric
identification,

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:57.440
metal detectors, cameras, video
analytics, vehicle barriers.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:01.220
And last but not least,
laser-based intrusion detection

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.090
systems.

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:03.490
That's right, laser-based.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:09.520
Building our own
hardware allows us

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:12.100
to create infrastructure
that precisely

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.770
meets our requirements,
including what we

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.470
need for delivering security.

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:21.510
Both our server boards as
well as our network equipment

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:24.070
are custom designed by Google.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.920
We carefully vet
component vendors

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:29.860
and work closely with
them to audit and validate

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:34.820
the security properties
provided by each component.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:39.080
Besides security, this is also
a benefit to our performance.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.290
So let me give you
an example there.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:44.740
In our latest generation
Jupyter network,

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:48.460
we have increased the capacity
of a single data center network

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.410
more than 100 times.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.870
Our Jupyter fabric
at the moment can

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:57.130
deliver more than 1 petabit
per second of total bisection

00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:58.120
bandwidth.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:00.700
To put this in other
words, the capacity

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:02.860
will be enough for
100,000 servers

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:07.450
to talk to each other with
10 gigabits second each.

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:11.360
Andromeda or the TPU
are other examples

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:13.880
where we have designed
custom solutions to achieve

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.940
much higher performance.

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:19.210
And now you may think,
well, this performance seems

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:22.930
impressive, but what does
it have to do with security?

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:26.590
And I will explain later
how this also allows

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.540
us to create better security.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:30.580
For example, think
about what that

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.620
might do for our
ability to mitigate

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:34.500
denial of service attacks.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.930
However, an example of
a purpose-built hardware

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:41.170
specifically for
security is Titan.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:43.930
It's a custom ship that we
created for establishing

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.460
a hardware route of trust.

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:48.265
It's also a personal
accessory for Urs.

00:12:48.265 --> 00:12:49.540
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.690
Titan is currently being
deployed on both servers

00:12:52.690 --> 00:12:54.190
and peripherals.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:55.960
And the ship allows
us to securely

00:12:55.960 --> 00:13:00.010
identify and authenticate
legitimate Google devices

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.240
at the hardware level.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:07.450
So in other words, with Titan
we have a strong, trusted route

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:10.640
for our boot process.

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:13.100
In general, our
server machines use

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.920
a variety of
different technologies

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:19.250
to make sure they are booting
the correct software stack.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:22.400
In all of this, we use
cryptographic signatures

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:26.800
over low-level components,
like the BIOS, the bootloader,

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:30.470
the kernel, and the base
operating system image.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:33.500
These signatures can be
validated during each boot

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:34.870
or update.

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:37.150
And all of these
components in this process

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.880
are Google-controlled,
built, and hardened.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:42.830
For each new
generation of hardware,

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:45.470
we strive to continuously
improve security.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.440
For example, depending on
the generation of the server

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.410
design, we root the
trust of the boot chain

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:55.880
in either a lockable firmware
chip, a microcontroller running

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:59.990
Google-written security
code, or as I just mentioned,

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:03.550
the Titan security chip.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:06.760
Due to our trusted
boot process, we

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:11.300
can provision unique
identities to each server

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:15.140
in the data center that can be
tied back to the hardware root

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:19.640
as well as the software with
which the machine was booted.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:21.800
The machine identity
is then used

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:25.970
to authenticate API calls to
and from low-level management

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:27.860
services on the machine.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:30.180
And let me illustrate
that point for you.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:35.230
Let's assume we suspected that
a machine was compromised.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:39.370
After reboot, the machine
comes back with an identity

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:42.790
only if all components
of the boot process

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.230
could be validated.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:46.780
As a result, the
successful reboot

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:49.300
indicates the machine is clean.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:53.020
Furthermore, we have
offered automated systems

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:55.150
to ensure a service
run up-to-date versions

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:58.450
of their software stacks,
including security patches.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.840
To detect and diagnose
hardware and software problems.

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:04.490
And to remove machines
from service, if necessary.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:12.510
Here's another important point.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:16.750
Within Google,
everything is a service.

00:15:16.750 --> 00:15:19.240
We have fundamentally
designed our platform

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:21.340
to be a multi-tenant system.

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:26.380
That is, we do not assume
any trust between services.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.830
So let me explain how this
works and how we secure

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:32.260
these services as a result.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:35.440
We focus on four
key areas here--

00:15:35.440 --> 00:15:41.470
service identity and isolation,
interservice access management,

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:44.140
encryption of interservice
communication.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:49.430
And last but not least, access
management of end user data.

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:53.650
So let's talk about service
identity and isolation first.

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:57.820
As I mentioned before, all
inter-process communication

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:01.540
is mutually authenticated
and authorized.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:05.280
It is important that we
provide strong access control

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:07.870
at an abstraction
level and granularity

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:11.800
that administrators and services
can naturally understand.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:15.520
For that purpose, we apply
cryptographic authentication

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:18.620
and authorization at
the application layer.

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:21.530
Because we can
enforce authorization

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:23.870
at the application
layer, we do not

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:26.690
rely on internal
network segmentation

00:16:26.690 --> 00:16:30.060
or filewalling as our
main security mechanisms.

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.320
I mean, however, to be clear,
we do use ingress and egress

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:35.300
filtering at various
points in our network

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.350
to prevent IP spoofing as an
additional security layer.

00:16:39.350 --> 00:16:41.660
This approach also
helps us maximize

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:47.860
both our network's performance
as well as availability.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:50.800
Each service that runs
on this infrastructure

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:53.950
is provided its own
cryptographic credentials

00:16:53.950 --> 00:16:56.560
that they can use to
prove their identity when

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:59.200
making or receiving RPCs.

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:01.930
This allows clients to
ensure that they are talking

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.599
to the intended
server, and allow

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:07.960
servers to limit access
to methods and data

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:10.359
only to specific clients.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:13.780
We also have a variety of
isolation and sandboxing

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:16.480
techniques for protecting
services from each other

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:18.170
when they run on
the same machine.

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:21.460
There is, obviously, regular
Linux process separation,

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:25.150
but we also have language- and
kernel-based sandboxes as well

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.069
as hardware virtualization.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:28.860
And the approach that
we undertake here

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:32.560
is that we use more isolation
layers for riskier workloads.

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:35.680
For example, when running
user-supplied code on products

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:39.990
like Google App Engine
or Google Compute Engine.

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:42.810
As an additional
security boundary,

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:46.470
we enable very sensitive
services, such as the cluster

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:49.680
orchestration service and
some key management services,

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:53.820
to run exclusively on
dedicated machines.

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:59.050
So let's go to the next one,
interservice access management.

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:02.760
Access management features
provided by the infrastructure

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:06.280
allow a service owner
to precisely specify

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:08.090
who can communicate with it.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:09.520
For example, a service.

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.890
Let's say a Bigtable
storage server,

00:18:11.890 --> 00:18:15.370
may want to offer
some APIs solely

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:18.520
to a specific
whitelist of services.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.320
The storage server
can then be configured

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.890
with a whitelist of the allowed
service account identities.

00:18:23.890 --> 00:18:26.800
And this access restriction
is then automatically enforced

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:28.930
by the infrastructure.

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:31.950
Google engineers
accessing services

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:34.870
are also issued
individual identities.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:37.840
So services can also be
configured specifically

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:40.660
to allow or deny their access.

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:44.140
All of these types of
identities-- machines, service,

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:45.220
and employee--

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:47.970
live in a global namespace
that is maintained

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:49.570
by our infrastructure.

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:51.940
This is separate from
end user identities.

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:55.330
I'll talk about them later.

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:59.920
This infrastructure also
provides a rich identity

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.520
management workflow that
includes approval chains,

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.350
logging, and notification.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:10.360
For example, an identity can be
assigned to an access control

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:15.250
group via a system that
allows for two-party control.

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:18.760
And by two-party control, I mean
that one engineer can propose

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.220
a change to the group,
but another engineer,

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:24.910
the admin of the group,
needs to approve that change.

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:29.290
Our system allows secure access
management processes that scale

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:33.130
to the thousands of services
running on the infrastructure.

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:36.940
On top of this automatic
API-level access control

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:39.370
mechanism, the
infrastructure also

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:42.370
allows services to
implement their own custom

00:19:42.370 --> 00:19:46.330
fine-grained access control by
giving the ability to a service

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:49.020
to check its central
ACL and group database.

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.250
So now, let's talk
about the encryption

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.830
of interservice communication.

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:02.220
Beyond authentication
and authorization,

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:05.880
the infrastructure also
provides cryptographic privacy

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:09.480
and integrity for RPC
data on the network.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:14.880
To extend these benefits to
other protocols, such as HTTP,

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:19.650
we encapsulate them inside our
infrastructure RPC mechanism.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:22.080
This gives application
layer isolation

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.290
and removes any dependency on
the security of the network

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:26.520
path.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:30.840
As a result, encrypted
interservice communication

00:20:30.840 --> 00:20:34.530
can remain secure even
if the network is tapped.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:39.390
And if you think back to
2013, the Snowden revelations

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:42.990
described the threat
of tapping in detail.

00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:46.500
To protect against such
sophisticated adversaries

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:50.190
trying to tap our private
interdata center links,

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:53.940
the infrastructure automatically
encrypts all infrastructure RPC

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:57.720
traffic that goes over the wide
area network between our data

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:01.590
centers without requiring
any explicit configuration

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:04.570
from the service.

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:07.840
Last but not least, let's
talk about access management

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:09.940
of end user data.

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:12.760
A typical Google service
is written to do something

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:13.570
for an end user.

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:15.820
For example, an
end user may want

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:18.310
to store their email on Gmail.

00:21:18.310 --> 00:21:21.280
And to provide a
complete service,

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:24.610
the Gmail service may
call an API provided

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:26.530
by the contact service.

00:21:26.530 --> 00:21:30.970
For example, to access an
end user's address book.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:34.630
Just a minute ago, I was talking
about how the contact service

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:38.890
can be configured such
that RPC requests are only

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:41.320
allowed from the Gmail service.

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:43.320
Plus, perhaps some
other services

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:46.080
the contact servers
may want to allow.

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:48.940
While this sounds like a
good place to begin with,

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:52.810
this may still result in a
very broad set of permissions.

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:56.320
For example, this may
grant the Gmail service

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:59.500
the ability to request
the Contacts of any user

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:00.180
at any time.

00:22:03.930 --> 00:22:07.780
However, as the Gmail
service makes an RPC request

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:12.320
to the contact service on
behalf of a particular end user,

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:14.340
we can do better.

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:17.210
Our infrastructure
provides the Gmail service

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:20.300
the capability of presenting
an end user permission

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:23.600
ticket as part of the RPC.

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:27.260
This ticket serves as proof
that the Gmail service

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.360
is acting on behalf of
a particular end user.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:32.690
And this allows
the contact service

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:35.780
to require such a
ticket on each RPC,

00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:38.240
and then return data
only for the end

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:40.780
user named in this ticket.

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:42.940
We have integrated
this capability

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:46.510
into our central user
identity service.

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:48.760
The central user
identity service

00:22:48.760 --> 00:22:53.140
requires a user credential, such
as a cookie or an OAuth token,

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:55.870
and returns a short-lift
end user permission

00:22:55.870 --> 00:23:00.940
ticket that can then be used
for subsequent RPCs related

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:02.320
to the user's request.

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.820
In our example,
the Gmail service

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:07.210
would request the end
user permission ticket

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:09.880
and would pass it down
to the contact service.

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:12.940
For any cascading call, the
end user permission ticket

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:15.070
can be handed down by
the calling service

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:18.120
to the callee as
part of the RPC call.

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:26.380
So let's go to the next
level of the stack.

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:29.590
And let's talk about
how data is stored.

00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:34.090
By default, all data is
encrypted in one or more ways

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:37.810
with no extra effort
from the user.

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:41.800
The way that this works is by
providing storage services,

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:46.510
such as Bigtable, that abstract
away the physical storage.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:48.760
Our storage services
then integrate

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:51.250
with a central key
management service

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.110
and can be configured
to use keys

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:56.140
from the central
KMS to encrypt data

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:59.670
before it's written to
the physical storage.

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:02.980
And I just told you about these
end user permission tickets.

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:06.400
The central KMS also
integrates with them,

00:24:06.400 --> 00:24:09.750
so that keys can be tied
to particular end users.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:21.090
The benefit from performing
encryption at the application

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:25.470
layer is that it also allows us
to isolate the infrastructure

00:24:25.470 --> 00:24:29.610
from threats at lower levels,
such as malicious disk

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.150
firmware.

00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:35.020
As you may recall, this is not
a theoretical threat either.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:38.010
Nonetheless, we also enable
hardware encryption support,

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:40.560
both on our hard
drives and SSDs.

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:43.200
And we also track each
drive very carefully

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.360
through its complete life cycle.

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:49.620
And if a de-commissioned drive
cannot be securely erased via

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:54.000
a multi-step process, the
drive is physically destroyed

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.520
on premise, for example,
just by shredding it.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.790
While the previous slide talked
about how we enable encryption

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:07.650
by default, we do provide other
options for our cloud customers

00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:12.330
based on their trust
level and business needs.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:17.130
For customers that need to
operate with minimal trust--

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:20.590
this is the very right-hand
side of the slide--

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:24.880
we support Customer-Supplied
Encryption Keys, CSEKs,

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:27.930
which enable them to maintain
their own separate root

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:31.020
of trust and push
keys at time of use

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.510
to Google Cloud via an API.

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:35.670
Those keys are
then stored in RAM

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:41.460
only during the time required to
perform the specific operation.

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:45.880
However, with CSEK the
burden and responsibility

00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:50.980
of protecting and not losing
the keys falls with a customer.

00:25:50.980 --> 00:25:55.120
Google has no way to recover the
customer's data if the keys are

00:25:55.120 --> 00:25:57.340
inadvertently deleted or lost.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:59.660
This is very easy to get wrong.

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:03.910
So any customer who wants
this minimal level of trust

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:07.990
and wants to use CSEK needs
to be exceedingly careful.

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:11.860
And must also invest in their
own key distribution system

00:26:11.860 --> 00:26:15.370
to push keys to Google to
match the rate at which they

00:26:15.370 --> 00:26:18.660
are being used in
their applications.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:21.510
Operationally, the
customer-supplied key

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:25.500
is pushed to Google Cloud,
a [INAUDIBLE] is added.

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:28.260
And instead of the
Google-maintained key,

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:30.990
encryption key, the
customer-supplied key,

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:33.450
including [INAUDIBLE],
is used as the resulting

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:35.430
key, encryption key,
for the customer's data.

00:26:39.482 --> 00:26:41.690
Another option-- and now
I'm talking about the middle

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:42.860
of the slide--

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:45.440
is our cloud key
management service

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:48.170
that we announced in the
keynotes this morning.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:52.490
Cloud KMS enables
customers to leverage

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:54.500
our globally scalable
key management

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:59.010
system while maintaining
control of key operations,

00:26:59.010 --> 00:27:02.750
including full audit
logging of their keys.

00:27:02.750 --> 00:27:05.570
This solution alleviates
the need for the customer

00:27:05.570 --> 00:27:07.460
to create their own
key distribution

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:10.430
system while still
enabling them to control

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:12.800
the visibility of their keys.

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:17.355
With Cloud KMS, keys created
and maintained in Cloud KMS

00:27:17.355 --> 00:27:19.730
are used as the
key encryption keys

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:23.190
in place of Google's
default key encryption keys.

00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:27.770
As you can see, we have several
different options for you

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:30.950
to choose from based on
your own levels of trust

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:33.110
and what is required
by your business.

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:39.550
In addition to providing
you with different choices

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:42.080
for encryption to
protect your data,

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:44.620
we have also created
a service that

00:27:44.620 --> 00:27:49.000
allows you to classify
sensitive data automatically.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.820
In February last year, we
announced the data loss

00:27:51.820 --> 00:27:53.710
protection features in Gmail.

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:57.460
This year, we have expanded the
application of this technology

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:01.240
to Drive and have also
published this as an API

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:04.350
for you to integrate
with yourself.

00:28:04.350 --> 00:28:06.300
And as you may have
seen earlier today,

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:10.180
there are many different ways
to leverage the DLP APIs.

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:14.980
They can be integrated into
mobile or support applications

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:17.810
so that data can be
classified and reacted

00:28:17.810 --> 00:28:19.470
to during time of use.

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:22.320
For example, you can
flag data containing PII,

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:24.480
such as government ID numbers.

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:27.000
Social security numbers would
be one example as well as

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:30.000
credit cards and many
other identifiers.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.850
Beyond protecting data
through encryption,

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.250
awareness of the
data content enables

00:28:35.250 --> 00:28:38.400
you to customize your
controls and take actions

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:43.381
based on the specific
sensitivity of the information.

00:28:43.381 --> 00:28:43.880
All right.

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:45.380
While we just took
a slight detour

00:28:45.380 --> 00:28:48.830
into ways in which you
can protect and analyze

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:52.580
your own data, let's get
back to the infrastructure

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:57.650
Google has built to enable
secure internet communication.

00:28:57.650 --> 00:29:01.490
Our infrastructure lives
on a private IP address

00:29:01.490 --> 00:29:04.520
space that is completely
isolated from the internet.

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:09.020
To bridge that gap, we have
created a single point of entry

00:29:09.020 --> 00:29:12.500
that we call the
Google Front End, GFE.

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:16.050
The GFE is a globally
distributed system,

00:29:16.050 --> 00:29:18.560
but it provides a
logical enforcement

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:22.280
point for all traffic from
the internet to Google.

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:25.370
In the context of a
multi-tenant infrastructure,

00:29:25.370 --> 00:29:28.280
it's important to
remember that GFE is just

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:33.300
another service that runs on
top of our infrastructure.

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:35.640
Before any service
can make itself

00:29:35.640 --> 00:29:37.980
available on the
internet, it first

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:40.920
needs to register
itself with the GFE.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:44.700
And the GFE then ensures
that all TLS connections

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:47.570
are terminated using
correct certificates

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:50.640
and follow best practices,
such as supporting

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:53.040
perfect forward secrecy.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:55.530
When the GFE
receives the request,

00:29:55.530 --> 00:29:58.230
it forwards it to the
corresponding service

00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:02.340
using the RPC security protocol
I just explained earlier.

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:05.700
From that perspective,
the GFE plays the role

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:08.280
of a smart reverse proxy.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:12.000
In addition to providing public
IP domain hosting and TLS

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:16.980
termination, GFE also provides
protection against denial

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.390
of service attacks.

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:21.540
For Cloud customers, we
offer similar capabilities

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:24.690
via the Google
Cloud Load Balancer,

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:27.450
which provides global load
balancing with anycast

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:30.750
IP, cross-region load
balancing, as well

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:33.840
as automatic
multi-region failover.

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:39.260
It also provides
TLS termination.

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:41.950
So let's drill into
protection for denial

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:43.510
of service attacks some more.

00:30:43.510 --> 00:30:46.600
And I'll explain to you
how this works for Google.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:50.710
Our first tier of defense
is that at the scale

00:30:50.710 --> 00:30:54.880
of our infrastructure, we can
simply absorb many DOS attacks.

00:30:54.880 --> 00:30:57.820
Remember when I talked about the
custom-build high-performance

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.050
network equipment?

00:30:59.050 --> 00:31:01.750
Here is where we also
realized the security

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:03.740
benefits of high performance.

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:08.380
However, we do have multi-tier,
multi-layer DOS protections

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:12.010
to further reduce the chance
that any DOS attack might

00:31:12.010 --> 00:31:14.800
impact services
running behind the GFE.

00:31:14.800 --> 00:31:18.140
And let me explain that to you
in a little bit more detail.

00:31:18.140 --> 00:31:19.900
Let's start from the top.

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:23.200
When the backbone delivers
an external connection

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:26.290
to one of our data
centers, this connection

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:30.190
passes through several layers of
both hardware and software load

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:32.050
balancers.

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:35.050
These load balancers
report statistics

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:39.106
about the incoming traffic
to a central DOS service.

00:31:39.106 --> 00:31:40.480
That was actually
the first thing

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:43.870
I worked on when I joined
Google 13 years ago.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:48.190
When it detects a DOS attack,
it can configure load balancers

00:31:48.190 --> 00:31:51.880
to drop or throttle traffic
associated with the attack.

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:55.120
At the next layer,
the GFE instances

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:58.200
also report information
about requests they receive

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:00.400
to this central DOS service.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.220
And this includes further
application layer information

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:06.190
that is not available
to the load balancers.

00:32:06.190 --> 00:32:08.830
And the central DOS
service then again

00:32:08.830 --> 00:32:12.850
can configure these GFE
instances to drop or throttle

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:14.850
traffic.

00:32:14.850 --> 00:32:17.340
Since the Google
Cloud load balancer

00:32:17.340 --> 00:32:21.840
is built on top of the GFE, you
can benefit from DOS mitigation

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:25.260
by placing your own
services behind [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:29.590
However, for customers that
need more fine-grained control,

00:32:29.590 --> 00:32:33.450
we also have a set of
trusted third-party partners

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:37.410
that provide DDoS mitigation,
appliances, and services.

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:45.340
After DOS protection,
the next layer of defense

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:48.420
comes from our central
identity service.

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:50.470
The central identity
service provides

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:52.630
a login page for
end users, asking

00:32:52.630 --> 00:32:54.730
for a username and password.

00:32:54.730 --> 00:32:58.720
It can also challenge a user
for additional information

00:32:58.720 --> 00:33:02.650
based on various risk
factors, such as the history

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:06.370
of previous login session from
a particular device or login

00:33:06.370 --> 00:33:09.260
location, for example.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:13.520
As we all know, humans often
remain the weakest link

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:14.930
in the chain.

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:17.690
It is just too easy
for us to fall victim

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:21.110
to a phishing attack or
to inadvertently install

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:23.750
malware that compromises
the computer,

00:33:23.750 --> 00:33:26.150
and then takes control over it.

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:30.920
And even today, it remains
true that for most companies,

00:33:30.920 --> 00:33:33.640
that spear phishing continues
to be the way in which they

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:34.820
get compromised.

00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:37.430
It's an incredibly
effective attack.

00:33:40.770 --> 00:33:44.550
And since humans are
the weakest link,

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:47.660
let's just use an example here.

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:50.950
Let's assume the infrastructure
was perfectly secure.

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:52.480
Everything is patched.

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:55.420
There are no exploitable
vulnerabilities.

00:33:55.420 --> 00:33:59.440
None of that security matters
if the adversary can just

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:01.930
come in through the front door.

00:34:01.930 --> 00:34:08.350
And since spear phishing attacks
remain exceedingly successful,

00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:11.380
once an admin credential
has been stolen,

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:14.889
the adversary can impersonate
the admin at any time

00:34:14.889 --> 00:34:18.159
to make arbitrary changes.

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:19.780
So what do we do about this?

00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:21.400
To make phishing
less effective, we

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:24.480
have pioneered the
U2F security key.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:27.820
The security key is a
hardware second factor

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:30.159
that can be associated
with an account

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:33.880
so that login is only possible
when the second factor is

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:36.810
present.

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:40.920
It is now also possible
for a domain administrator

00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:44.670
to mandate a security key
for accounts in her domain.

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:48.060
We call this feature
security key enforcement.

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:50.460
I also mentioned malware before.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:54.040
A malware compromise can
also be quite problematic.

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:57.990
For example, assume the
laptop of an administrator

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:00.330
was compromised.

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:04.080
As a result, the malware can
effectively make any changes

00:35:04.080 --> 00:35:06.030
at once to a cloud deployment.

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:08.680
In some sense, it's game over.

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:11.130
So what can we do about that?

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:13.460
The best practice
is really to choose

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:15.870
a client platform
that's inherently

00:35:15.870 --> 00:35:18.120
designed for security.

00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:21.780
If an administrator was
solely using a Chromebook

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:24.600
running Chrome OS,
the malware compromise

00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:26.760
would be exceedingly
less likely,

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:30.000
as Chrome OS is designed for
security from the ground up

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:34.330
and does not allow the
installation of software.

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:38.860
My recommendation to everyone
is to institute a policy that

00:35:38.860 --> 00:35:42.340
effectively states
admin access is only

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:45.070
allowed if the admin
comes from a Chromebook

00:35:45.070 --> 00:35:47.744
with a security key.

00:35:47.744 --> 00:35:51.070
So because I believe
in this so strongly,

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:53.110
let me reiterate that.

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:57.430
The best practice is to use a
Chromebook with a security key.

00:35:57.430 --> 00:36:02.080
I suggest that you also turn
on security key enforcement.

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:05.530
Many of you may not know what
security key enforcement is.

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:09.400
That's probably because we
just announced it this morning.

00:36:09.400 --> 00:36:13.300
With security key enforcement,
customer administrators

00:36:13.300 --> 00:36:18.310
on Google Cloud can now specify
that their employees or users

00:36:18.310 --> 00:36:22.210
must be strongly authenticated
with a security key.

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:26.330
This is a security practice that
we have mandated for employees

00:36:26.330 --> 00:36:28.790
at Google for many, many years.

00:36:28.790 --> 00:36:31.630
So now you might
say requiring this

00:36:31.630 --> 00:36:34.820
for all employees
may not be practical.

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:36.760
However, with security
key enforcement,

00:36:36.760 --> 00:36:43.270
it is also possible
to enforce least--

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:44.120
sorry.

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:46.730
With security key
enforcement, it's

00:36:46.730 --> 00:36:49.550
possible to enforce
these restrictions

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:51.830
on your high-risk
groups, such as admins,

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:55.130
which may be a compromise
for some deployments.

00:36:55.130 --> 00:37:00.800
As a side note, since this is
a domain administrator feature

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.320
in which account recovery--

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:05.600
account recovery when
a security key is lost

00:37:05.600 --> 00:37:08.900
becomes much easier.

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:11.830
So up to now, we have
discussed security properties

00:37:11.830 --> 00:37:13.270
of our infrastructure.

00:37:13.270 --> 00:37:15.730
And in the following,
I'll shed some light

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:18.670
on our operational practices.

00:37:18.670 --> 00:37:23.320
We make a large investment in
monitoring the client devices

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:26.200
that our employees use to
operate our infrastructure.

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:29.270
For example, we ensure
that the operating system

00:37:29.270 --> 00:37:31.990
images for these client
devices are up to date

00:37:31.990 --> 00:37:34.000
with security patches.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:38.080
We also control the applications
that can be installed and have

00:37:38.080 --> 00:37:41.080
systems for scanning content
for security problems

00:37:41.080 --> 00:37:46.570
related to user-installed apps,
downloads, browser extensions,

00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:49.390
as well as content
browsed from the web.

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:53.320
Being on the corporate LAN
is not our primary mechanism

00:37:53.320 --> 00:37:55.330
for granting access.

00:37:55.330 --> 00:37:58.210
We instead enforce
and manage access

00:37:58.210 --> 00:38:00.140
at the application level.

00:38:00.140 --> 00:38:03.850
This allows us to expose
internal applications

00:38:03.850 --> 00:38:08.590
to specific users only when
they are strongly authenticated

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:11.440
and are coming from a
correctly managed device

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:14.920
from expected networks
and geographic locations.

00:38:14.920 --> 00:38:17.590
We call this
approach "BeyondCorp"

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:21.790
and have talked about
it extensively before.

00:38:21.790 --> 00:38:25.480
Again you may rightfully
ask, how does all of this

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:26.290
apply to you?

00:38:26.290 --> 00:38:28.720
So let me try to explain.

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:33.660
The previously mentioned Cloud
KMS as well as the Google Front

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:37.300
End are examples of key
abstractions at the core

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:39.280
of Google's architecture.

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:42.130
We pursue the approach
that where possible,

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:45.780
we design our infrastructure
to be logically centralized

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:46.990
at manage.

00:38:46.990 --> 00:38:49.180
This makes it easier
to globally reason

00:38:49.180 --> 00:38:51.640
about the state of
the infrastructure.

00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:54.820
One key benefit of having
a logically centralized

00:38:54.820 --> 00:38:58.270
abstraction is that we can
then create virtual enforcement

00:38:58.270 --> 00:39:01.120
points in the
infrastructure to enforce

00:39:01.120 --> 00:39:04.330
policy and leverage
the underlying globally

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:07.090
distributed physical
infrastructure to implement

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:09.330
them.

00:39:09.330 --> 00:39:11.850
This morning, you may have
heard our announcement

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:15.510
of the Identity
Aware Proxy, IAP.

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:20.490
IAP makes it easy for you to
put an application on the cloud

00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:23.670
and configure a central
policy in IAP that

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:26.550
ensures that only the
right set of identities

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:29.070
get access to your applications.

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:32.410
In the context of logically
centralized infrastructure,

00:39:32.410 --> 00:39:35.730
IAP becomes your central
enforcement point

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:39.630
that gives you the ability
to assert policy on access

00:39:39.630 --> 00:39:41.910
to any of your applications.

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:44.820
It helps close the
problem of policy gaps

00:39:44.820 --> 00:39:46.800
due to large
complex deployments.

00:39:51.480 --> 00:39:53.600
And you remember
when I said that you

00:39:53.600 --> 00:39:57.520
should have a policy that allows
admins only from a Chromebook?

00:39:57.520 --> 00:40:00.800
I expect that we will give
that to you as a policy option

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:02.510
IAP in the future.

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:09.670
In summary, Google's
infrastructure

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:14.080
is designed to build,
deploy, and operate services

00:40:14.080 --> 00:40:16.780
securely at infinite scale.

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:18.970
Our Google Cloud
offerings are built

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:21.950
on top of that very
same infrastructure.

00:40:25.170 --> 00:40:27.270
The security of
the infrastructure

00:40:27.270 --> 00:40:30.720
is designed in layers, starting
from the physical components

00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:34.030
and data centers to
secure boot, secure

00:40:34.030 --> 00:40:38.010
interservice communications,
secure data at rest,

00:40:38.010 --> 00:40:41.400
protected access to
services from the internet,

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:43.770
as well as the
processes we deploy

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:46.320
for operational security.

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:48.420
Many of these
security capabilities

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:51.570
are available on Google
Cloud to you by default.

00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:55.860
We also continue to further
externalize our proven security

00:40:55.860 --> 00:41:00.390
capabilities and make them
available to US customers.

00:41:00.390 --> 00:41:02.985
Today, we announced
five of these--

00:41:02.985 --> 00:41:10.230
IAP, Cloud KMS, DLP,
security key enforcement,

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:12.560
and shared VPC.

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:16.490
And these join previous product
and open source security

00:41:16.490 --> 00:41:19.140
innovations with
many more to come.

00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:21.050
So please, stay tuned.

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:22.820
Thank you very much.

00:41:22.820 --> 00:41:24.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:25.820 --> 00:41:29.170
